// Deal or Force - Movement.
//
// How fast an army walks, and what slows it down. Every key here overrides the value
// compiled into Defines.cs; a key no file mentions keeps its compiled value, so a mod only
// lists what it changes and never has to fork this file.
//
// These numbers used to exist only in the source. They are here because "how fast do armies
// move" is the first thing anybody wants to retune and the last thing they should have to
// read C# to find.
//
// The march is one multiplication, and every term in it is listed below or beside it:
//
//   progress per day
//     = MarchProgressPerDay        this file
//     x unit speed                 common/unit_types
//     x stance                     this file
//     x supply, floored            this file
//     x river crossing             00_combat.json, RiverMovementMultiplier
//     x terrain                    common/terrain
//     x rail redeployment          this file, when being railed
//     x roads                      this file: 1 + RoadSpeedPerInfrastructure per level of
//                                  infrastructure in the region entered, up to RoadSpeedCap
//
// Progress accumulates until it reaches 1, and then the formation is in the next province.
// So 0.75 a day is a day and a third to cross one - and a province here is a district,
// not a country, because there are thirty-eight thousand of them.
//
// Taking ground that belongs to somebody else is a different sum and lives in 00_combat.json:
// UnopposedCapturePerDay, ZoneOfControlResistance and OrderedAdvanceFloor between them decide
// how fast an advance moves through a defended country, and they matter far more to the pace
// of a war than anything in this file.
{

  // The march
  //
  // Raising MarchProgressPerDay alone is the obvious change and the one to be careful with:
  // the 2024 front is chaotically sensitive to it. At 0.27 Ukraine keeps 56 % of its ground
  // after two years of nobody playing, at 0.32 it keeps 75 %, and the bench's stalemate check
  // fails at both. Armies feeling right is not one number - it is a pass over entrenchment,
  // defence and how a front absorbs a breakthrough. Change it in a mod and see for yourself.
  //
  // 0.22 until 0.6.3.0: four and a half days a district read as a crawl against the hourly
  // clock. At 0.75 (with open country and ordered advances doubled below) the 2024 front over
  // 8 seeds ends with Ukraine at 128 provinces on average against 102 - more mobile, and a
  // seed in eight now collapses where none did.
  "MarchProgressPerDay": 0.75,

  // What supply can cost a march, at worst.
  //
  // Supply multiplies pace directly, and a formation whose trace has been cut sits at 0.06 -
  // so without a floor, walking out of your own supply range would mean seventy days to a
  // province, which is not slow but stopped. This is the number that decides what an advance
  // through enemy country actually runs at, because that is exactly where the trace fails.
  "MarchSupplyFloor": 0.45,

  // Stance
  "MarchCautiousMultiplier": 0.86,
  "MarchAggressiveMultiplier": 1.14,

  // The railways: faster, and worse when it arrives. The organisation is charged per day of
  // travel, and entrenchment is lost outright - a formation that has been railed somewhere
  // arrives quickly and in no condition to attack on the day it gets there.
  "RailRedeploymentSpeed": 3.2,
  "RailRedeploymentOrganisationPerDay": 0.16,

  // Roads. Infrastructure was counted for supply, trade and production and not for the one
  // thing everybody means by roads: a division crossing the Ruhr and one crossing the Gobi
  // marched at the same pace. A region seeds with one to four levels, so a march there runs
  // ten to forty per cent faster; building more is how a player makes an army quicker at home.
  "RoadSpeedPerInfrastructure": 0.1,
  "RoadSpeedCap": 0.6,

  // Open country. Ground nobody is within FrontCoverageRadius of - the country behind a
  // broken line, a province the enemy never garrisoned - is taken at this pace instead of
  // UnopposedCapturePerDay, with supply counted at no less than OpenCountrySupplyFloor. A
  // gap in a line the enemy is still holding stays slow; what lies behind it no longer takes
  // three weeks a district.
  "OpenCountryCapturePerDay": 0.6,
  "OpenCountrySupplyFloor": 0.7,

  // Explicit orders into covered but unoccupied ground. These do not bypass a garrison,
  // combat, terrain or rivers; the autonomous planner's coverage refusal is unchanged.
  // Worst-case supply and coverage must not multiply a single district into months.
  "OrderedCapturePerDay": 0.5,
  "OrderedCaptureSupplyFloor": 0.7,
  "OrderedAdvanceFloor": 0.5
}
